<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/earth-interior">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    We live on the skin of a machine we have never seen. No drill has passed about 12 km — the
    Kola Superdeep Borehole in Russia reached 12,262 m in 1989 and still holds the record — and
    that is a pinprick through a 6,371 km radius, like piercing an apple a fraction of a
    millimetre deep. Everything deeper is known the way doctors know hearts: by listening. When
    earthquakes ring through the planet, <strong>seismic waves</strong> speed up, slow down,
    bend or vanish depending on what they cross; reading those signals from thousands of
    stations worldwide maps the interior in astonishing detail — down to slabs of old ocean
    floor sinking through the lower mantle today.
  </p>
  <p>
    Temperature is the second spy. The graph below is the <strong>geotherm</strong>: how hot it
    gets as you descend. The near-surface climb (about 25–30 °C per km — miners have always
    known their pits get hot) steepens through the crust, then the curve's <em>dips and
    kinks</em> around 400 and 660 km are not pauses in heating but mineral phase changes, where
    olivine's atoms reorganise under pressure and briefly absorb energy. Read it to the bottom:
    the inner core sits near 5,400 °C — about the temperature of the Sun's visible surface. The
    coolest place on the whole journey is where you are.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the geotherm — temperature from crust to core; note the dips around 400–660 km" widget="plotter" config={geothermCfg} />

  <h2>The layers, two ways of slicing</h2>
  <p>
    Geologists slice the planet twice, and keeping the two schemes apart is the key to the
    whole subject. By <em>chemical composition</em>: <strong>crust</strong>, <strong>mantle</strong>,
    <strong>core</strong>. By <em>mechanical behaviour</em> — rigid versus able to flow —:
    <strong>lithosphere</strong>, <strong>asthenosphere</strong>, and the deeper zones. The
    crust is the thin skin: ocean floors are 5–10 km of dense basalt, continents 30–70 km of
    thick, buoyant granite. The mantle runs to 2,890 km and is solid rock — olivine-rich
    peridotite — but <em>solid that flows</em>: like glass in old windows or asphalt on a hot
    day, it creeps a few centimetres per year, and that slow convection is the engine of plate
    tectonics. The core, from 2,890 km down, is iron and nickel. The outer core is liquid — its
    churning generates Earth's magnetic field — and the inner core (from 5,150 km) is solid
    iron, <em>hotter</em> than the liquid above it but crushed by pressure past iron's melting
    point. Pressure, not temperature alone, decides state.
  </p>
  <p>
    A sanity check ties the layers to arithmetic: the whole Earth's average density is about
    5.5 g/cm³, while surface rocks are 2.7–3.0 — so the deep interior must be dense iron-rich
    material. That simple weighing, before any seismology, is what told scientists the core
    was metal.
  </p>

  <h2>How the numbers were won</h2>
  <p>
    The seismological detective story ran in three famous steps. In 1909 Andrija Mohorovičić
    noticed that earthquake waves arriving far from a quake had jumped in speed — they had
    crossed into faster rock below a boundary we now call the <strong>Moho</strong>, the
    crust's base. In 1914 Beno Gutenberg fixed the core boundary near 2,890 km, and the
    <strong>S-wave shadow zone</strong> proved the outer core liquid: S-waves are shear waves,
    and shear cannot cross liquid, so they vanish entirely on the far side of the planet from
    big quakes beyond about 103° of arc — leaving a ring of "shadow" stations that hear
    nothing. In 1936 Inge Lehmann, studying the faint echoes that <em>did</em> arrive inside
    the P-wave shadow, discovered a solid inner core reflecting within the liquid one — a
    discovery made from a single Danish station and superb reasoning.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two more lines of evidence close the case. Waves reflect off boundaries like sonar
    echosounding, and arrival times now fix layer depths to within kilometres. And
    <strong>meteorites are samples</strong>: iron meteorites are fragments of shattered
    planetesimal cores, thought to resemble Earth's own core in composition (iron with nickel
    and heavier siderophile elements) — the only "core specimen" anyone will ever hold.
  </p>

  <h2>Wegener's drifting continents</h2>
  <p>
    In 1912 Alfred Wegener, a German meteorologist, revived an old observation as a grand
    claim: the continents move. His book <em>The Origin of Continents and Oceans</em> (1915)
    assembled the evidence. The coastlines fit — South America's east coast nests into Africa's
    west like torn paper (a fit later confirmed by computer at the continental shelf edge).
    Identical fossils — the seed fern <em>Glossopteris</em>, the freshwater reptile
    <em>Mesosaurus</em> — appear on now-distant shores but nowhere in between, impossible to
    explain by an animal swimming a salt ocean. Mountain belts match: the Appalachians line up
    with the Scottish Caledonides across the Atlantic. And glacial scars in bedrock appear in
    today's tropical India and Brazil, pointing as if ice had flowed outward from a shared
    south-polar cap that no present arrangement allows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wegener was right about the drift and wrong about the engine — he suggested continents
    ploughed through ocean crust like ships through ice, and physicists correctly showed that
    was impossible. The rejection was near-total for half a century; then the 1950s–60s
    delivered the missing machinery. Mapping the seafloor revealed a global mountain chain
    (the Mid-Atlantic Ridge) with young rock at its crest; <strong>magnetic stripes</strong>
    recorded on either side (Vine and Matthews, 1963) showed new seafloor forming at the ridge,
    cooling through reversals of Earth's magnetic field like a tape recorder, and spreading
    symmetrically outward. Continents were not ploughing through anything: they ride
    <em>within</em> plates that are themselves gliding on the flowing mantle. Plate tectonics
    became the unifying theory of geology.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the plates are not "the continents". A plate is a slab of
  rigid lithosphere — crust <em>plus</em> the topmost mantle — up to a couple of hundred
  kilometres thick; continents ride inside plates like logos on a moving conveyor belt, and
  oceanic plates form, move and get destroyed entirely independently of them.</p>

  <h2>Plates and their three boundaries</h2>
  <p>
    Earth's rigid shell is broken into about a dozen major plates, and almost all the
    interesting geology — volcanoes, great earthquakes, mountain ranges — happens at the three
    kinds of edge between them.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Divergent boundaries</strong> pull plates apart. Mantle rock rises beneath the
    thinning plate and, released from pressure, partly melts (<em>decompression melting</em>),
    welding new basaltic ocean floor onto both plates. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is the slow-motion
    version: the Atlantic widens a couple of centimetres per year — roughly your fingernails —
    and Iceland, straddling the ridge, is the one place you can walk on it. Continental rifting
    (the East African Rift) is the same process starting on land, and geologists expect it to
    eventually split Africa and open a new ocean.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Convergent boundaries</strong> bring plates together, and what happens depends on
    the crust involved. Where an ocean plate meets another ocean plate, the older, denser one
    dives beneath — <em>subduction</em> — building volcanic island arcs like Japan and the
    Marianas, and the planet's deepest trench (Challenger Deep, nearly 11 km). Where ocean
    meets continent, the ocean plate subducts beneath the continent: the Peru–Chile trench and
    the Andes, whose volcanoes exist because water released from the descending slab lowers
    the melting point of the mantle above (<em>flux melting</em>) — continents are fed by
    water-driven volcanism. Where continent meets continent, neither can sink far: the crust
    crumples and thickens into the Himalaya, still rising as India continues its
    50-million-year collision with Asia.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Transform boundaries</strong> slide plates past each other — no magma, no mountains,
    but enormous friction and earthquakes. The San Andreas Fault, slicing California, is the
    textbook case: the Pacific plate slips northwestward past North America at about 5 cm per
    year, in sticks and jerks that are each an earthquake. Transform edges are also why the
    famous mid-ocean ridges look like stitched zippers: offset by transform faults the whole
    way along.
  </p>

  <h2>Hot spots and mantle plumes</h2>
  <p>
    Not all volcanism sits on plate edges. Some sits on <strong>hot spots</strong>: narrow
    columns of unusually hot rock — mantle plumes — rising from deep in the mantle, melting
    the plate above it from below. The evidence is that hot spots stay (nearly) fixed while
    plates glide over them, so they burn a hole a volcano at a time and leave an
    <em>age-progressive chain</em>. The Hawaiian chain is the classic: active Kīlauea sits over
    the plume, and volcano ages march steadily to the northwest along the 6,000 km chain,
    reaching tens of millions of years old at its far, eroded end — the plate's motion,
    recorded in rock. Yellowstone sits over a continental hot spot that has left a chain of
    calderas across Idaho; Iceland is a plume sitting fortuitously on a ridge, double the
    volcanism. Hot spots also anchor a subtle measurement: because they barely move, they
    provide the reference frame against which plate motions are actually measured.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    What actually drives the plates? Textbook "convection" is only half the story. Modern
    force budgets put most of the push–pull on the plates themselves: <strong>slab pull</strong>
    — the weight of the cold, dense subducting slab dragging its plate — dominates, contributing
    perhaps 90% of the driving force, with <strong>ridge push</strong> (gravity sliding off the
    high, hot ridge crest) a secondary term. Mantle convection is therefore entangled with the
    plates: they are not cargo on a conveyor but part of the moving machine. Seismic tomography
    now images sinking slabs ("slab graveyards") piled near the core–mantle boundary, and
    plumes can be traced at least partway down — though their exact depth of origin remains
    genuinely debated.
  </p>
  <p>
    The core keeps its own mysteries. The geodynamo that generates our magnetic field runs on
    convecting liquid iron, and the field's polarity <em>flips</em> — north becomes south — at
    irregular intervals averaging a few hundred thousand years (the last reversal, the
    Brunhes–Matuyama, was 780,000 years ago). The flips are recorded in cooling lavas and
    spreading seafloor, which is precisely the "tape" Vine and Matthews read. Meanwhile the
    inner core, newly appreciated to be anisotropic (seismic waves run faster pole-to-pole
    than along the equator), may rotate at a slightly different rate than the rest of the
    planet — a result that has swung back and forth in the literature over the last 25 years
    and is still contested. Earth generates about 47 terawatts of heat — roughly half from
    radioactive decay, half primordial — and that budget, leaking out of the floor you stand
    on, is the original battery behind everything in this chapter: the mantle's flow, the
    plates' drift, the geotherm's climb toward the Sun's surface temperature at the centre of
    the world.
  </p>
  <p>
    The field those convecting currents make is not a decoration. It deflects the solar wind —
    the Sun's constant outflow of charged particles — carving the magnetosphere that makes
    Earth's atmosphere possible at all (Mars, which lost its dynamo ~4 billion years ago, lost
    much of its air afterwards, possibly not by coincidence). Charged particles funnel along
    field lines into the poles and excite the air they strike: the aurora is the magnetic
    field, made visible. Compasses, bird migration, and the magnetic stripes of the seafloor
    all read the same dynamo — one more way the planet's unvisitable interior reaches up and
    touches the surface.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Wegener, <em>The Origin of Continents and Oceans</em> (1915); Vine & Matthews, Nature (1963); OpenStax, <em>An Introduction to Geology</em>, chapters 2 and 10; USGS earthquake science; standard geotherm models.</p>
